oido-dictate (0.1.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Better diagnostics when the microphone can't be opened. Previously the worker
    crashed with a raw traceback that the Speak window hid behind "engine stopped
    unexpectedly", so a mic/audio problem looked like a model-load crash.
    - oido_worker.py now catches the audio-input open failure and reports a clear
      "no microphone found" / "cannot open microphone" message (with the available
      input device names) instead of an unhandled PortAudioError.
    - stt.py (Speak window) shows the engine's real error on an unexpected exit,
      including an uncaught traceback's last line, instead of a generic message.
    - oido-doctor: when ALSA sees a capture device but the app can't open it (a USB
      headset stuck in PipeWire's "Pro Audio" profile hides its mic), it now says so
      and prints the fix: wpctl set-profile <card-id> 1.

 -- Tarel <334372633+tarelerulz@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 21 Nov 2026 01:10:00 +0000

oido-dictate (0.1.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: typing was slower than in earlier builds, and text did not land natively in
    web apps (Gmail in Firefox). Two causes, both fixed:
    - inject.py defaults to the native Wayland input-method daemon (imetype, same path as
      an on-screen keyboard), but the daemon was not in the package, so every phrase
      silently fell back to key-by-key wtype. imetype is now shipped in
      /opt/oido-dictate/bin and inject.py finds it there.
    - oido-speak still forced DICTATE_METHOD=type (key-by-key), overriding that default,
      because the imetype binary used to be unshipped. It now defaults to the native
      input-method path (ime), with automatic fallback to wtype for windows/sessions that
      do not speak text-input, so dictation into Firefox/Gmail is as fast as a plain editor.
    - oido_worker.py now also finds the shared inject.py beside itself on a clean install,
      not only in the author's ~/moonshine-v2 dev tree.

 -- Tarel <334372633+tarelerulz@users.noreply.github.com>  Sat, 21 Nov 2026 00:05:00 +0000

oido-dictate (0.1.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: debian/copyright said MIT for the whole package. The bundled CPU engine comes from
    lokutor-ai/oido, which is GPL-3, so the package is now declared GPL-3 (the gpu-port-kit
    code stays available under MIT). 0.1.1-0.1.3 shipped the wrong licence text.
  * No code changes: binaries, worker and kit are identical to 0.1.3.

 -- Tarel <334372633+tarelerulz@users.noreply.github.com>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 23:00:00 +0000

oido-dictate (0.1.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New engine oido-hybrid (GPU add-on, Pi 4/400): a long phrase (>= 4.5 s) with a real
    pause is cut there; the GPU transcribes the head while the CPU (cores 1-3, nice 19)
    transcribes the tail. Any CPU problem falls back to the whole phrase on the GPU.
    Default engine is still oido-gpu-lm.
  * Pi 5 (and other dotprod CPUs): the CPU engine binary tasr_cli_dotprod_static is rebuilt with
    4x4 sdot GEMM, 4-row sdot attention and NEON softmax/activation/im2col/dequant. Logits are
    bit-identical to the old build; ~1.5x faster on 3 Cortex-A720 cores (Pixel 9 VM stand-in),
    NOT yet measured on a real Pi 5.

 -- Tarel <334372633+tarelerulz@users.noreply.github.com>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 22:00:00 +0000

oido-dictate (0.1.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: 0.1.1 changed encoder outputs slightly (not bit-exact), which could change
    LM-beam text on some clips. Logits are bit-identical to 0.1.0 again.
  * Still faster than 0.1.0 on the CPU engine (4x4 NEON GEMM, H*T attention split).

